'''Alngith''' ({{respell|al|-|ngeeth}}) is an [[language extinction|extinct]] [[Paman languages|Paman language]] formerly spoken on the [[Cape York Peninsula]] of [[Queensland]], [[Australia]], by the [[Alngith people]]. The last known speakers survived into the 1980s.<ref name=":0">Wurm, S. A. and S. Hattori (eds.). 1981. Language atlas of the Pacific area. Canberra: The Australian Academy of the Humanities in collaboration with the Japan Academy.</ref> [[Phonology|Phonologically]], this language variety is very similar to the related variety [[Linngithigh dialect|Linngithigh]], the only difference being that this language has the process of [[metathesis (linguistics)|metathesis]] whereas Linngithigh does not.<ref>Kenneth Hale, 1976, Phonological Developments in Particular Northern Paman Languages, pp.12-13</ref>

Alngith is considered to be a dialect of Thaynakwith, a language spoken on Western Cape York in the Weipa area taking in Albatross Bay and Mission River. The traditional language area of Thaynakwith includes landscape within the local government boundaries of [[Weipa]] Town Council and the [[Shire of Cook]].<ref>{{SLQ-CC-BY|url=https://www.slq.qld.gov.au/discover/aboriginal-and-torres-strait-islander-cultures-and-stories/languages|title=Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander languages|access-date=18 January 2023}}</ref>

The word ''Nje 'alan'' means 'good day'.{{Citation needed|date=March 2026}}

==Phonology==
The two dialects of Alngith and Linngithigh have the same sound inventory.<ref>Kenneth Hale, 1976, Phonological Developments in Particular Northern Paman Languages, pp.14</ref>

